          "question": "Which drug classes does heart failure guidance describe for reduced ejection fraction?",
          "answer": "The retrieved guidance confirms that heart failure with reduced ejection fraction has its own set of \"foundational\" drug classes, but the text I received does not list them. The passage cuts off just before the list.\n\nHere is what the retrieved passages do say:\n\n- **Heart failure guidance (educational summary, \"Heart failure with reduced ejection fraction: the four foundational therapy classes\")** defines reduced ejection fraction as 40 percent or below. It describes four foundational classes, started together or in quick succession, but the names are not in the text I have.\n- **The same document** warns that these classes should not be applied automatically to preserved ejection fraction. It notes that the SGLT-2 inhibitors are the newest addition and the one class shown to help in preserved ejection fraction.\n- **Type 2 diabetes guidance (national diabetes association standards of care, educational summary)** says that agents with proven organ benefit are preferred when heart failure is present, \"particularly with reduced ejection,\" but that passage is also cut off before naming the agents.\n\nTo get the full list, the complete heart failure guidance document would need to be checked. A clinician can also go through the classes and how they fit a particular situation.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
